./LOGBOOK.md:286:- CUSTODY: Prompt-free `/usr/bin/codesign` probes succeeded for the named Apple Development and Developer ID Application identities; the named `relux-works-notary` profile is in the login Keychain; the owner retained the source notarization private-key file as owner-controlled recovery material outside repo/board/logs; Sparkle 2.9.4 generated the EdDSA private key into login-Keychain custody.
./LOGBOOK.md:287:- PRIVACY: Targeted post-ceremony scans found zero secret/key-path/key-ID/issuer-ID/account-credential candidate files in the Git-scoped repository, board resources, run logs, and shell history, and zero key files in repository/board scope. History nevertheless contains one safe Sparkle generation command-name line and one safe Keychain-unlock command-name line, with no password flags; full history suppression therefore cannot be claimed.
./LOGBOOK.md:393:- FINDING (wider than the verdict): the **ACTIVE** register was worse and no verdict had reported it. R26 required `reviewedExceptions[].reviewedIn` to be **non-empty and nothing else**, so it accepted `2026-07-28.r99` — a revision never issued, the exact case the conditional register had rejected since r11. That register's entry authorises the Sparkle Mach-lookup exception that **is** in the shipped macOS host bundle.
./LOGBOOK.md:533:- ROOT CAUSE: r2 argued in D-4 that `ReluxProxyMac` is sandboxed *because of* Sparkle, and quoted the spec clause naming the `-spks`/`-spki` Mach lookup exceptions — then never authored `com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name` as an entitlement row. The r2 containment assertion A10b declares every present key must be authored or signing-generated, so the allowlist would have **rejected a correctly built sandboxed Sparkle host**. Same defect class as r1's F3, one revision later: prose acknowledged the requirement, the machine-checkable set did not. The generalisable lesson is that the moment an assertion becomes *exhaustive*, every architectural consequence stated anywhere in the contract must also appear as data — rationale text is no longer a place a requirement can live.
./LOGBOOK.md:535:- ANOMALY: **Tailscale carries the Sparkle exception while `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` is `false`** — the same product cited as D-4 evidence that hosts are routinely unsandboxed. The exception is inert without the sandbox, so "shipping products carry this key" is not by itself evidence that a key is required. Worth remembering when mining installed apps for entitlement precedent: presence proves the shape is *accepted*, never that it is *needed*.
./LOGBOOK.md:536:- FINDING: Sparkle 2's framework binary names **three** endpoint suffixes — `-spks`, `-spki`, `-spkp` — not two. The spec entitles two because the Downloader, Installer Connection, and Installer Status XPC services stay disabled. Recording this makes the two-value set legible as a deliberate least-privilege choice rather than an omission the next reviewer re-derives.
./LOGBOOK.md:537:- DECISION: The row is attributed `project-architecture`, **not** `sandbox-consequence`, even though `com.apple.security.network.client` on the same target is a sandbox consequence and is equally Sparkle-driven. `network.client` follows mechanically from sandboxing alone; this key needs two independent project decisions to both hold (ADR-018's Sparkle choice **and** the host sandbox), so it is not derivable from "this target is sandboxed". Also added **rule X1**: the spec bans "unreviewed exception entitlement", so the contract now carries a reviewed-exception register plus an explicit banned set for Hardened Runtime relaxations — `get-task-allow` deliberately excluded from that set, since it is toolchain-injected on the development channel and governed by A10d instead. Without X1 this defect recurs one key at a time.
./LOGBOOK.md:547:- DECISION: `ReluxProxyMac`'s sandbox is recorded as a **project** decision — `.spec/platform-distribution.md` embeds Sparkle only in the sandboxed hardened-runtime host with `SUEnableInstallerLauncherService` and the `-spks`/`-spki` Mach exceptions, per ADR-018 — not an Apple rule. The contract now carries `requirementSource` per row (`apple-requirement` / `project-architecture` / `sandbox-consequence` / `platform-inherent`) and validator R21 rejects a host row claiming an Apple requirement.
./LOGBOOK.md:602:- DECISION: Ceremony C1 split. C1 is permission grants only (Keychain unlock, portal auth, identifier/profile authorization, notarytool profile, Sparkle keygen). The probe build `1r0fxv` and its independent review sit **outside** it, and Approval A1 (one click on the probe's system-VPN dialog) is a separate later interaction. The previous version claimed a human could remain present through a producer→reviewer cycle.
./LOGBOOK.md:604:- DECISION: Sparkle key generation separated from updater integration (ADR-026). `ziprhs` may run at C1 because it depends on nothing; `SUPublicEDKey` pinning and appcast sign/verify became `TASK-260728-3bj9bk`, blocked by `uyju7n` → `xempiv` and `1mt4e7`. Accepting the key ceremony no longer implies a working self-update signing path.
./LOGBOOK.md:675:- DECISION: Pin official Sparkle 2.9.4 exactly in the macOS host; require pre-extraction EdDSA verification plus a signed feed with failure expiration disabled; publish only final Developer ID-signed, hardened-runtime, notarized/stapled DMGs from a public read-only HTTPS origin after asset-first verification. Stable is Sparkle's default channel and opt-in prerelease uses `prerelease`; private GitHub remains manual distribution.
./LOGBOOK.md:676:- CORRECTION: Sparkle 2 removed downgrade support, so emergency rollback is last-known-good source rebuilt at a higher `CFBundleVersion`, not an explicit rollback channel. Sparkle also documents Developer ID key rotation, so downstream tests must enforce the approved product identities instead of asserting every Team ID change is intrinsically rejected by Sparkle.
./LOGBOOK.md:677:- PLATFORM: Apple TN3134 requires the directly distributed macOS packet tunnel to be a system extension. Sparkle host replacement/relaunch does not activate the new provider; the host must stop the tunnel, relaunch, submit a separate replacement request, surface approval/restart state, and avoid automatic reconnect. Exact OS behavior remains a physical gate under `TASK-260715-1r48pc` and `TASK-260715-2aessv`.
./LOGBOOK.md:681:- FINDING: The seed threat model stated several accepted designs as shipped mitigations even though the repository has no concrete packet-tunnel provider target, selected production SSH adapter, Keychain vault, DNS/route composition, relay deployment/frame-pump composition, degraded-mode controller, platform fail-closed implementation, Sparkle path, or application release pipeline.
